    $astroNeb_master$> We have measured the system and met the body that rules it. Now we ask where all of it came from — and this chapter begins the origin story. The claim is that the Sun and every planet, moon, asteroid, and comet condensed, four and a half billion years ago, from **a single cold, dark cloud of gas and dust** collapsing under its own gravity. It was first proposed on pure reasoning — by Immanuel Kant in 1755, who looked at the *shape* of the solar system and deduced its origin — and it is now supported by direct photographs of the same process around other stars. Everything turns on one question: **why does a collapsing cloud produce a flat, spinning disk rather than a simple sphere?** The answer is the conservation of angular momentum, and it is the reason the solar system is flat. We will also meet a genuine unsolved problem sitting at the theory's heart — the Sun holds 99.87% of the mass but only 0.61% of the angular momentum — and end with the evidence, which is spectacular: in 2014 we photographed a planet-forming disk around a star less than a million years old. **Kant deduced the disk in 1755; we took its picture in 2014.** That is the arc of this chapter.

## A deduction in 1755, a photograph in 2014

In 1755 a thirty-one-year-old private tutor in Königsberg named Immanuel Kant — not yet the towering figure of modern philosophy — published a book of cosmology that asked a question almost nobody had thought to ask: *why is the solar system shaped the way it is?*

Kant had noticed what any reader of eighteenth-century astronomical tables could notice but few had tried to explain. The planets do not orbit in random directions, as independently captured objects would. Instead: **all the planets orbit the same way** around the Sun; **all orbit in very nearly the same plane** — the system is flat, like a dinner plate, not spherical like a swarm of bees; **the Sun itself rotates that same way**, its equator near that plane; and **the orbits are nearly circular**, not wildly elliptical. Four independent regularities, all pointing the same way. Kant reasoned they could not be coincidence — and proposed that **the entire system condensed together, out of a single vast rotating cloud of diffuse matter, which flattened as it contracted.** The planets share a plane and a direction because they inherited the rotation of the cloud that made them. Forty-one years later Pierre-Simon Laplace developed a mathematically richer version, and it became the **nebular hypothesis.**

Consider what Kant had to work with. No telescope worth the name. No spectroscopy — composition would soon be declared permanently unknowable. No knowledge of gravity's behaviour in gas, no nuclear physics, no idea what the Sun was made of, no concept of the interstellar medium. He had a table of orbital elements and the refusal to accept four coincidences. From that alone he deduced, correctly, that the solar system condensed from a rotating disk.

{{image: HL Tauri | The protoplanetary disk around HL Tauri, imaged by the ALMA array in 2014 — a flat, rotating disk of gas and dust crossed by concentric bright rings and sharp dark gaps, where planets appear to be sweeping their orbits clear. The star is astonishingly young, less than a million years old. This is a direct photograph of the structure Immanuel Kant deduced in 1755 from nothing but the shape of the solar system — and its ringed detail carried a shock: planet formation, it seems, begins almost immediately.}}

Now jump forward two hundred and fifty-nine years. In 2014 the Atacama Large Millimeter/submillimeter Array — sixty-six radio dishes on a high Chilean plateau, working as one instrument — was pointed at **HL Tauri**, a young star about 450 light-years away and **less than a million years old**, still embedded in the remains of its birth cloud. The image ALMA returned is one of the most consequential astronomical photographs in decades: a **flat, rotating disk** crossed by **concentric bright rings separated by dark gaps**, like a vinyl record. The most natural reading is that **planets are already forming in those gaps**, each sweeping its neighbourhood clear. This mattered for two reasons. It was a direct photograph of the thing Kant deduced. And it was a genuine shock, because HL Tauri is *so young* — planet formation was expected to take much longer to carve structure that clean. **Planets, it appears, begin forming almost immediately.** This chapter is about the physics in between: how a cold dark cloud becomes a spinning disk with a star at its centre — and why that outcome is not merely possible but very nearly inevitable.

## The raw material: giant molecular clouds

Before anything collapses there must be something to collapse. The space between the stars is not empty — it holds the **interstellar medium**, thin gas (mostly hydrogen) with a trace of dust, on average about *one particle per cubic centimetre*. But it is not uniform. Here and there it clumps into colder, far denser concentrations called **giant molecular clouds**, and these are where stars are born. Their properties are worth sitting with:

- **Temperature: 10–20 K** — within twenty degrees of absolute zero, colder than anything in the solar system outside a laboratory. Cold enough that hydrogen exists as *molecular* H₂, hence the name.
- **Density: 10² to 10⁶ particles per cubic centimetre.** This figure is double-edged. Compared with the average interstellar medium these clouds are thousands to millions of times denser — genuinely dense, for space. But compared with the air you are breathing (about $2.7 \times 10^{19}$ per cm³), a cloud at 10⁴ per cm³ is roughly **a thousand trillion times thinner than air** — a far better vacuum than any laboratory can make. Star formation happens in what we would call a near-perfect vacuum; only *gravity* and *enormous size* make it work.
- **Size: tens of light-years across** — vastly larger than any planetary system.
- **Mass: 10³ to 10⁷ solar masses** — enough to make thousands or millions of stars. And they do: stars form in *clusters*, not singly.
- **Composition: ~99% gas, ~1% dust.** That 1% — microscopic grains of silicate, carbon, and ice — is small but crucial: it is the raw material of every rocky planet, and it is what makes the clouds *dark.*

{{image: Barnard 68 | The dark molecular cloud Barnard 68 — a cold, dense knot of gas and dust so thick that it blots out the light of every star behind it. To nineteenth-century observers such objects looked like empty tunnels through the Milky Way; William Herschel called one "a hole in the heavens." They are the opposite of holes: the densest structures in interstellar space, and the birthplaces of stars. Because their dust extinguishes visible light, star formation must be watched at infrared and submillimetre wavelengths — which is why ALMA and JWST have transformed this subject.}}

Dust grains absorb and scatter visible light very effectively, so a giant molecular cloud seen against a rich star field appears as a *hole* — a region where the background stars simply vanish. These are the **dark nebulae**, which to nineteenth-century eyes looked like empty tunnels; Herschel famously called one "a hole in the heavens." They are the opposite: the densest structures in the interstellar medium, so thick with dust that they extinguish everything behind. This has a defining practical consequence: **star formation happens where visible light cannot reach.** To watch stars being born you must observe in the infrared, submillimetre, and radio — which is exactly why ALMA and JWST transformed this field, and why the HL Tauri image was possible at all.

## Why the cloud collapses: gravity against pressure

A molecular cloud sits in a contest. **Gravity pulls inward** — every part attracts every other, and left alone would collapse the cloud to a point. **Thermal pressure pushes outward** — the gas is warm enough that its particles' motion resists compression, like air in a balloon. (Turbulence and magnetic fields help support real clouds too, but the thermal argument captures the essence.) Which wins is set by the **Jeans criterion**: a cloud collapses if its mass exceeds a critical value, the Jeans mass, which scales as

$$M_J \propto \frac{T^{3/2}}{\rho^{1/2}}$$

Read that carefully, because it explains the entire geography of star formation. The Jeans mass *rises* with temperature (hotter gas has more pressure, so more mass is needed for gravity to win) and *falls* with density (denser gas has stronger self-gravity). Therefore **collapse is easiest in gas that is cold and dense** — which is exactly what a giant molecular cloud is. This is why stars form in cold molecular clouds and essentially nowhere else; the warm, diffuse medium simply cannot collapse. The 10–20 K temperature is not incidental — it is the enabling condition.

```example
jeans-collapse
```

Clouds often sit near the dividing line, and something usually has to *trigger* collapse by compressing a region past threshold — a nearby supernova's shockwave, passage through a galactic spiral arm, a cloud–cloud collision, or the winds of nearby massive stars. And for *our* system there is striking forensic evidence of the trigger. Certain meteorites contain the decay products of **short-lived radioactive isotopes** — notably aluminium-26, with a half-life of about **717,000 years**. These are forged in massive stars and scattered by supernovae. Now weigh what a 717,000-year half-life implies: after a few million years, essentially none is left. So if aluminium-26 was present in the material that made the solar system — and the decay products in meteorites show unambiguously that it was — **it must have been created shortly before our formation and injected into our cloud.**

```example
halflife-decay
```

The inference is remarkable: **a massive star died nearby, and its debris — perhaps along with the shockwave that triggered the collapse — became part of the Sun and planets.** Our solar system may owe its existence to the death of a star we will never identify, and we carry that star's chemical fingerprint in rocks that fall from the sky. (Aluminium-26 had a second role: its decay was a *major heat source* in the early system, hot enough to melt the interiors of the first planetesimals and drive them to form cores and mantles — the dying star supplied the furnace too.) Once collapse begins in earnest it is fast: the free-fall time depends only on density, and for a dense clump it is only a few hundred thousand years. Against the 4.6-billion-year age of the solar system, **star formation is essentially instantaneous** — which is why finding structured, planet-forming rings around sub-million-year-old HL Tauri is so startling.

## Why a disk? The physical heart

We arrive at the central question. Gravity pulls equally in all directions, so the naive expectation is a *sphere* shrinking uniformly — yet the solar system is emphatically flat. Why? The answer is **rotation** and the conservation of **angular momentum**.

Start with an observation: essentially every molecular cloud has *some* rotation. It need not be much — clouds are turbulent, buffeted by shocks and galactic shear, and the chance of *exactly* zero net rotation is nil. That small initial spin turns out to be decisive. **Angular momentum** — for our purposes $L = m r^2 \omega$ — is conserved absent external torque, and you have seen the consequence: a figure skater spins slowly with arms out, pulls them in, and accelerates dramatically without pushing off anything. Her angular momentum is fixed, so shrinking $r$ forces $\omega$ up; since $L \propto r^2\omega$, halving the radius *quadruples* the spin. This is not new physics — it is the same conservation law behind Kepler's second law, the equal-areas rule, operating on a collapsing cloud instead of an orbiting planet. Apply it to a cloud a light-year across turning once per million years, and the spin-up is enormous:

```diskcollapse
```

Now the crucial step. **Rotation does not oppose gravity equally in all directions — it opposes it only in the plane perpendicular to the rotation axis.** Consider the two directions separately. *Along the axis* (toward the poles), material has no rotational motion about the axis; nothing resists gravity, so it **falls freely inward** and the cloud contracts rapidly in that direction. *In the equatorial plane*, material is rotating, and as it moves inward it spins faster (as we just saw), strengthening the outward centrifugal effect; eventually it reaches a radius where rotation balances gravity and **inward collapse halts** — the material settles into orbit rather than falling further. Put those together and the outcome is inevitable: **a rotating cloud collapses freely along its axis but is halted by rotation in its plane, so it flattens — sphere to ellipsoid to pancake — and settles into a rotating disk.**

```example
spin-up
```

This is not fine-tuned; it is what *must* happen to any collapsing cloud with any rotation at all — which is to say, essentially every cloud. **Disks are not accidents; they are the generic outcome of gravitational collapse with angular momentum.** And this single consequence explains all four of Kant's regularities at once: the planets share a direction and a plane because they formed from one disk's rotating material; the Sun rotates that way because it formed at the disk's centre; the orbits are near-circular because disk material in rotational balance already moves on near-circular paths, and collisions damp what eccentricity remains. Four independent facts, one cause. That is the mark of a good theory — it explains things it was not invented for. It even explains a fifth: why the *system* is flat but the *Sun* is a sphere. The Sun holds so little of the total angular momentum that rotation barely deforms it; the disk material, holding almost all of it, could not collapse further and stayed flat.

## Where the mass goes: making a star at the centre

The disk explains the system's *shape*; we also need its *mass distribution* — why 99.87% ended in one central body and only a whisper remained in orbit. The same asymmetry does it. Material falling along the axis meets no rotational barrier and reaches the centre; disk material, in rotational balance, must first *lose* angular momentum before it can spiral in, and does so only slowly. So a **protostar** grows at the centre, fed by polar infall and the disk's inward drain, heating as gravitational energy converts to heat, glowing long before fusion begins. When the core reaches about 10 million kelvin, hydrogen fusion ignites, pressure balances gravity, contraction halts, and a **star** is born. **The Sun is the collapsed cloud; the planets are the leftovers** — the fraction with too much angular momentum to fall in. This block is largely the study of the residue.

{{image: Herbig–Haro object | A Herbig–Haro object — glowing beams of gas blasted out along a newborn star's rotation axis at hundreds of kilometres per second, slamming into the surrounding cloud. These bipolar jets are launched during the violent T Tauri phase of a young star's life, in the one direction not blocked by the disk. They matter twice over: they help destroy the disk's gas within a few million years, setting the deadline for planet formation — and they carry angular momentum bodily out of the system, part of the answer to why the Sun spins so slowly.}}

The newborn star does not settle quietly. Young stars pass through a violent **T Tauri** phase — intense magnetic activity, enormous flares, winds far stronger than today's solar wind — and many drive spectacular **bipolar jets** along the rotation axis (the one direction the disk does not block). This matters enormously, because it is how the disk's gas is eventually destroyed: between the star's fierce winds, its ultraviolet light, and radiation from nearby massive stars, the gas is heated and blown away. Observations of young clusters show protoplanetary disks disperse in roughly **three to ten million years.** That number is the deadline: any planet that wants a massive hydrogen–helium atmosphere must acquire it before the gas is gone. We will lean on it constantly in the next chapters.

## The angular momentum problem: where the theory strains

I have presented the nebular hypothesis as a clean success, and in outline it is. But a genuine, quantitative problem sits at its centre, recognized for over a century — and this course has a standing commitment to being honest about what is unsettled. The naive expectation is that the Sun, the collapsed centre of a spinning cloud, should hold a large share of the system's *angular momentum*, just as it holds nearly all the mass. It does not:

```angularbudget
```

**The Sun holds 99.87% of the mass but only 0.61% of the angular momentum** — the distribution is almost exactly inverted. Jupiter, a tenth of a percent of the mass, carries **61%** of the angular momentum, a hundred times the Sun's share. Recall the spin-up: collapse increases rotation as $1/r^2$ over an enormous range of radii, so had the Sun kept the angular momentum of the material that formed it, it would be spinning near the limit at which it would tear itself apart — or at least turning in hours. It is not; it turns once in a leisurely **25 days.** So the material that became the Sun must have **shed almost all of its angular momentum** on the way in. To what, and how?

The modern resolution is that angular momentum was **transported outward** — by several mechanisms believed to contribute together. **Magnetic braking:** the young Sun's field threads the ionized disk and, because field lines resist twisting, acts like elastic tethers dragging angular momentum from the fast-spinning star out to the slower disk (the modern solar wind still brakes the Sun slightly today). **Disk turbulence:** instabilities let angular momentum flow outward while mass flows inward, so some material spirals in to feed the star while other material is pushed out carrying the spin. **Bipolar jets:** the T Tauri outflows carry mass and angular momentum bodily out of the system. Are these enough? They are widely believed to be the right ingredients, and detailed models using them can reproduce slow-rotating stars — **but the problem is not settled in detail.** And that is worth stating as a general lesson: **a theory can be overwhelmingly correct in its main claims and still carry a serious unsolved problem at its core.** The nebular hypothesis is not in doubt — we photograph the disks it predicts — yet the angular momentum distribution required a century of extra physics and is not fully resolved even now. This is what a healthy, live science looks like from the inside — exactly like the coronal-heating problem of the last chapter.

## The evidence: photographs of the process

For two centuries the nebular hypothesis was an inference from architecture. It is now something stronger, because we can *watch it happen* around other stars. In the 1990s the Hubble Space Telescope turned on the Orion Nebula and found dozens of small dark disks silhouetted against its glow — **protoplanetary disks**, seen directly around newborn stars, soon nicknamed **proplyds.** Their message was immediate: disks are *common, not exceptional* — Kant's inference described a universal stage of star formation. The 2014 ALMA image of **HL Tauri** went further, resolving the internal structure of a disk around a sub-million-year-old star into rings and gaps — and carrying the shock that **planet formation begins almost immediately.** And lest HL Tauri seem unique, the **DSHARP** survey (2018) imaged twenty disks and found rings, gaps, spirals, and asymmetries almost everywhere: substructure is the norm. More recently JWST has begun reading the *chemistry* of these disks — water, carbon dioxide, organics in the inner regions where terrestrial planets form — spectroscopy applied to planet formation in progress. And older systems show **debris disks**, the collisional dust left after formation is largely done, as around Beta Pictoris, where a planet has since been directly imaged.

We cannot watch any single system from start to finish — it takes millions of years — but we can assemble the sequence from many systems caught at different stages: collapsing cores, embedded protostars, gas-rich disks, gapped planet-forming disks, and debris disks. **Kant deduced the disk from four numbers in a table of orbits. We now have photographs of disks at every stage of the process, around hundreds of stars.** The nebular hypothesis is no longer a hypothesis in the ordinary sense; it is an observed sequence — with the angular momentum problem as its most stubborn remaining puzzle.

## The cloud, the spin, and the flat solar system

Gather what this chapter established. Kant, with no telescope and no physics, read four regularities in the solar system's architecture and deduced a rotating, flattening cloud; ALMA photographed exactly that around HL Tauri. Stars form in giant molecular clouds — 10–20 K, tens of light-years across, thinner than air yet dense enough, by their sheer scale, for gravity to win; dark because dust extinguishes starlight, which is why the subject awaited infrared and submillimetre eyes. Collapse is governed by the Jeans criterion, $M_J \propto T^{3/2}/\rho^{1/2}$, so it happens in cold, dense gas and nowhere else; for us, aluminium-26 in meteorites betrays a nearby dying star that seeded and perhaps triggered our cloud. The physical heart is why collapse makes a disk: every cloud rotates, angular momentum is conserved, so the cloud spins up as $1/r^2$, and rotation halts collapse only in the equatorial plane — sphere becomes pancake, inevitably, explaining all four of Kant's regularities at once. The centre becomes the Sun; the planets are the residue; the T Tauri phase then blows the gas away within 3–10 million years. And the theory carries a real open problem — the Sun's 99.87% of mass but 0.61% of angular momentum — that transport mechanisms address but have not fully closed.

**Here is the idea to carry forward. The flatness of the solar system is not a curiosity but a fossil.** Every diagram of the planets arrayed in a plane is the preserved signature of a rotating cloud that could not collapse in its own equatorial plane four and a half billion years ago. The orderly architecture that let Kepler find laws and Newton explain them exists because angular momentum is conserved. And the disk that cloud left behind had structure — and above all a **temperature gradient**, hot at the centre and cold at the edge. That gradient is where the snow line finally gets its full physical treatment, and it is the subject of the next chapter.$astroNeb_master$,
